text:
ivy: Heads-up - support ticket where customer's production Next.js Edge build crashed with 'Request is not a constructor' only after we bumped SDK. They have several transitive deps that add a fetch polyfill. Need a quick diagnostic to send back.

sam: This keeps happening when a node polyfill gets bundled into an edge runtime. Short repro: build with a plugin that injects whatwg-fetch or undici/polyfill-global -> deploy to Vercel/Cloudflare -> runtime has native fetch + polyfill -> constructor identity mismatch.

kevin: We should give them a tiny diagnostic they can run locally. I drafted a one-file script that looks for multiple Request/Response constructors and prints the likely culprit. Something like:

```js
// fetch-inspector.js (node)
const entries = Object.keys(globalThis).filter(k => /Request|Response/.test(k));
console.log('global keys (subset):', entries.join(', '));
try {
  console.log('globalThis.Request === (globalThis as any).fetch?.Request?', globalThis.Request === (globalThis.fetch && globalThis.fetch.Request));
} catch(e) { console.log('check failed', e.message); }
// also print package tree entries that reference polyfill packages
require('child_process').exec('npm ls whatwg-fetch undici whatwg-url --json', (err, out) => console.log(out || err && err.message));
```

marta: Nice. Also include a tiny in-browser/edge snippet they can paste into a console to inspect constructor IDs: `console.log(window.Request === fetch.prototype?.constructor)` won't always work but `console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(new Request('/')).constructor.name)` helps. That shows you if two Request constructors differ.

ivy: Can we add quick remediation lines to the diagnostic output? Eg: if npm ls shows whatwg-fetch, print "remove polyfill for edge builds - scope to node only" and provide a sample vite/next.config change.

sam: Yes. For Vite: mention disabling node polyfills plugin or adding `optimizeDeps.exclude`/`ssr.noExternal` entries so the polyfill isn't bundled for edge. For Next.js: avoid `polyfillImports` or move polyfill import behind `if (typeof window === 'undefined')` in server-only entry. Also a one-liner: `grep -R "polyfill-global\|whatwg-fetch\|undici/polyfill-global" node_modules` to find offenders.

kevin: TypeScript note in the ticket: duplicate types (lib.dom + undici) cause cryptic TS errors like 'Request is not assignable to Request'. Quick fix: change our public types to a narrow alias: `export type SDKFetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;` and accept `fetch?: SDKFetch` in options. Suggest that in the reply.

marta: Also add our recommended code snippets: (1) sync-safe helper for bundles that doesn't import undici, (2) async resolver for node that dynamically imports undici. Provide both so customers can pick based on bundler.

ivy: I'll put together a minimal reply template with: 1) run fetch-inspector, 2) paste console snippet, 3) try removing polyfill and redeploy, 4) fallback: pass globalThis.fetch into client constructor. Want me to include the diagnostic script as a gist link?

sam: Yes include gist. Also add a short line about streaming: if they see 'getReader is not a function' they probably have a Response polyfill without web streams - require undici/node18+.

kevin: Final nit: remind them not to import undici/polyfill-global in edge builds and to prefer supplying fetch explicitly in CJS bundles (require('undici').fetch).

marta: I'll update the SDK FAQ entry with the diagnostic steps + the two helper functions + sample Vite/Next fixes and attach the fetch-inspector gist.

ci-bot: Created internal ticket #FF-4921 "support: fetch constructor mismatch - add diagnostic script + FAQ update" and linked PR draft. CI: smoke tests queued. :white_check_mark:
